  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1033 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nStronger lift will arrive Wednesday morning as the Texas gulf coast \nridge slides east with the western US trough slowly moving eastward \nacross the Intermountain West. Severe chances are unclear for \nWednesday with expected ongoing precipitation and expected cloud \ncover. The most pressing issue, however, will be the threat for \nheavy rainfall through the day on Wednesday and on Thursday. \nMoisture will definitely not be an issue for precipitation chances \nfrom Wednesday through Thursday. Periods of lift are expected along \nwith an intruding cold front which is difficult to pinpoint at this \ntime scale in the forecast. The threat for heavy rainfall on Tuesday \nafternoon/evening appears to be minimal given that storms are \nexpected to be moving quick enough to prevent widespread heavy \nrainfall. Precipitable water values were already near maximum values \nfrom latest raobs and these values are expected to only climb higher \nover the next few days. Mean precipitable water values from ensemble \nguidance is near or exceeds two inches in some areas off the caprock \nwhich is already a high amount for summer months. But this is \nOctober, which places these values in record territory. These values \ndrop to around 1.50 inches near the I-27 corridor but these values \nstill exceed the 99th percentile for October. Furthermore, mixing \nratios at 850mb will surge to near 16 g/kg by Tuesday afternoon. \nThis amount of moisture is due to the low level flow from the gulf \ncombined with mid and upper level moisture from Hurricane Simon off \nthe coast of western Mexico. All that being said, the main forecast \nissue will be timing of lift to act upon this abundant moisture from \nWednesday morning through Thursday afternoon. With the expected \ncloud cover and precipitation, temperatures have been adjusted \ndownward from blended guidance on Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nModels now show fairly good agreement in pinching off an upper low \nacross the southwestern US from this trough as the remainder of the \ntrough gets swept up with another trough dropping southeast through \nthe Great Lakes. It is this Canadian trough that will provide us \nwith our first strong cold front of the season. Exact timing of the \nfront is uncertain given that it is still at least 5-6 days away. \nNonetheless, the front will bring strong winds its immediate wake. \nModels are depicting a 1040mb surface ridge behind this front \ndropping into the Plains Friday and Saturday. Current forecast \nmorning temperatures next weekend are in the 40s with 30s in the \nsouthwestern Texas Panhandle but could likely drop lower with \nsubsequent forecasts given the strength of this surface high.",
